Q: How to add chars to a malloc buffer? A: <p>I think maybe you want to grow the allocated memory as needed. Try this:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;

void quit(const char *msg) {
    if (msg) fprintf(stderr, &quot;%s\n&quot;, msg);
    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
}

// read from /dev/urandom until two 0x00 in a row
int main(void) {
    FILE *r = fopen(&quot;/dev/urandom&quot;, &quot;rb&quot;);
    if (!r) quit(&quot;unable to open /dev/urandom&quot;);
    char *buffer = malloc(512);
    if (!buffer) quit(&quot;no memory&quot;); /* OS closes the file! */

    size_t blocksize = 512, bytesused = 0;
    int ch1, ch2 = 0xff;
    for (;;) {
        ch1 = ch2;
        ch2 = fgetc(r);
        if ((ch1 == ch2) &amp;&amp; (ch2 == 0)) {
            break; // stop reading at first double zero
        }
        buffer[bytesused++] = ch2;
        if (bytesused == blocksize) {
            // grow buffer
            size_t newblocksize = (blocksize * 13) / 8; /* Fibonnaci FTW */
/*DBG*/     printf(&quot;growing buffer from %zu to %zu bytes\n&quot;, blocksize, newblocksize);
            char *tmp = realloc(buffer, newblocksize);
            if (tmp == NULL) {
                fclose(r);
                free(buffer);
                quit(&quot;Not enough memory. Program aborted.&quot;);
            }
            buffer = tmp;
            blocksize = newblocksize;
        }
    }
    printf(&quot;read a total of %zu characters from /dev/urandom\n&quot;, bytesused);
    printf(&quot;final allocated buffer with %zu bytes\n&quot;, blocksize);
    fclose(r);
    free(buffer);
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to correctly use SetWindowsHookEx &amp; CallNextHookEx A: <p>According to the docs, the proper thing to do is pass the arguments you received directly to CallNextHookEx, exactly as you received them. You should also call CallNextHookEx regardless of whether you decided to handle the hook message.</p>
<p><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-gb/windows/win32/api/winuser/nf-winuser-callnexthookex" rel="nofollow noreferrer">According to MSDN</a>, the first parameter to <code>CallNextHookEx</code> is ignored on on NT/XP/2003, and for older Win95-based operating systems it should be the <code>HHOOK</code> you received when you registered your hook with <code>SetWindowsHookEx</code>. The docs don't specify a value for Windows 2000, but since it's part of the NT family, a reasonable guess is that it's ignored there as well.</p>
<p>Given all that, a good way to code the method for NT-family operating systems might be this:</p>
<pre><code>LRESULT CALLBACK CBTProc( int code, WPARAM wp, LPARAM lp )
{
    if( code == HCBT_CREATEWND )
        ProcessCreateWnd( wp, lp );
    return CallNextHookEx( 0, code, wp, lp );
}

void ProcessCreateWnd( WPARAM wp, LPARAM lp )
{
    // my code here
}
</code></pre>
<p>This makes sure that you always call the hook at the end of your processing, and makes it hard to accidentally add a <code>return</code> that will bypass <code>CallNextHookEx</code>.</p>

Q: OpenIdConnect signin-oidc route not handled by ASP.NET MVC A: <p>So, in the latest version (4.2.3) of <code>Microsoft.Owin.Security.OpenIdConnect</code> (in .net 4.8) you need to set the <code>RedeemCode</code> property on <code>OpenIdConnectAuthenticationOptions</code> to <code>true</code> for middleware to automatically handle the code exchange. Until then, it would search the <code>&quot;id_token&quot;</code>.</p>
<p>This is (a little simplified) code I'm currently using:</p>
<pre><code>app.UseOpenIdConnectAuthentication(new OpenIdConnectAuthenticationOptions()
{
    Authority = options.Authority,
    ClientId = options.ClientId,
    ClientSecret = options.ClientSecret,
    CallbackPath = new PathString(options.CallbackPath),
    CookieManager = new SystemWebCookieManager(),
    ResponseType = OpenIdConnectResponseType.Code,
    // OWIN does not trigger code exchange automatically - RedeemCode = true will fix this.
    RedeemCode = true,
    Scope = string.Join(&quot; &quot;, OpenIdConnectScope.OpenIdProfile, OpenIdConnectScope.Email),
    RequireHttpsMetadata = true,
    UseTokenLifetime = false,
    UsePkce = options.UsePkce,
    Notifications = new OpenIdConnectAuthenticationNotifications()
    {
        AuthorizationCodeReceived = ctx =&gt;
        {
            if (ctx.TokenEndpointRequest != null)
            {
                // OWIN leaves TokenEndpointRequest.RedirectUri empty - Keycloak will return invalid_grant.
                // Set the same redirect_uri as in the authorization request.
                var host = ctx.Request.Uri.Host;
                var portStr = options.HttpsPort.HasValue ? $&quot;:{options.HttpsPort.Value}&quot; : string.Empty;
                ctx.TokenEndpointRequest.RedirectUri = $&quot;https://{host}{portStr}{options.CallbackPath}&quot;;
            }
            logger.Log($&quot;AuthorizationCodeReceived: Code={ctx.Code != null}, TokenEndpointRequest.RedirectUri={ctx.TokenEndpointRequest?.RedirectUri}&quot;);
            return Task.FromResult(0);
        }
    }
})
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>I have been debugging this issue past two days. At first I used the solution of setting the <code>ResponseType</code> to <code>&quot;code id_token&quot;</code> and using the hybrid flow. Then I also needed to fetch the AccessToken and it won't work. The solution can be setting the response type to <code>&quot;code id_token token&quot;</code> but it seems wrong and would degrade security of this solution.
So I dig into the current source code <a href="https://github.com/aspnet/AspNetKatana/blob/main/src/Microsoft.Owin.Security.OpenIdConnect/OpenidConnectAuthenticationHandler.cs" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a> and found the <code>RedeemCode</code> property. Setting this on fixed the whole code-exchange issue.</p>
